select m.id, 2, $L$Requests & Responses: How the Web Talks$L$, $L$14 minute read$L$, $L$Every API interaction is a request and a response. Understanding their anatomy is the foundation of everything else in this course.$L$, array[$L$Break a request into its parts (method, URL, headers, body)$L$, $L$Break a response into its parts (status, headers, body)$L$, $L$Read a real request/response exchange$L$], $L$## The request has four parts
1. **Method** — the action: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE
2. **URL / endpoint** — what you're acting on
3. **Headers** — metadata: `Content-Type`, `Authorization`, `Accept`
4. **Body** — the data you send (mostly POST/PUT/PATCH), usually JSON

## The response has three parts
1. **Status code** — did it work?
2. **Headers** — metadata about the response
3. **Body** — the returned data (usually JSON)

## Reading an exchange
`GET /orders/42` → `200 OK` + a JSON order. `POST /orders` with a body → `201 Created`. Each request is **stateless** — it carries everything the server needs.$L$, $L$Request: `POST /orders` with `Content-Type: application/json` and body `{"item":"mug","qty":2}`. Response: `201 Created`, header `Location: /orders/99`, body `{"id":99,"item":"mug","qty":2,"status":"pending"}`. Check: status is 201, body echoes input, an id was assigned.$L$, $L$- Ignoring headers — auth and content-type problems hide there
- Only checking the status code and never the body
- Forgetting the body must match the Content-Type you declared$L$, $L$Always inspect the full response — status, headers and body. Many "passing" API tests only checked the status code and missed a wrong payload.$L$, $L$Write out the four parts of a request to fetch user 7 from `https://api.example.com`.$L$, $L$Which part of the response tells you whether the request succeeded?$L$, $L$Which part of the response tells you whether the request succeeded? (Answer: the status code)$L$, array[$L$I can name the parts of a request$L$, $L$I can name the parts of a response$L$, $L$I can read a request/response exchange$L$]
