select m.id, 5, $L$API Testing vs UI Testing$L$, $L$11 minute read$L$, $L$API and UI testing answer different questions. Knowing which to use — and when — is what separates a strategic tester from a button-clicker.$L$, array[$L$Contrast API and UI testing$L$, $L$Choose the right layer for a given check$L$, $L$Explain why the UI can mask API problems$L$], $L$## Different questions
- **API testing** asks: *is the logic and data correct?*
- **UI testing** asks: *can a user actually do this on screen?*

## Trade-offs
| | API | UI |
|---|---|---|
| Speed | Fast | Slow |
| Stability | High | Lower |
| Setup | Low | Higher |
| User realism | Lower | High |

## The UI can hide API bugs
A screen might show "Success" while the API returned the wrong data. Only testing the API reveals it.

## Rule of thumb
Push logic, data and edge-case checks **down** to the API. Keep the UI for a few true end-to-end journeys.$L$, $L$A discount API returned `£0.00` off for a valid code, but the UI defaulted to full price with no error — so UI testing "passed". An API test asserting the discounted total caught it immediately.$L$, $L$- Doing everything through the UI because it "feels real"
- Assuming a green UI means the data underneath is correct
- Never testing the API independently of the screen$L$, $L$When the UI and the API disagree, trust the API response — then find out why the UI hid the truth.$L$, $L$Give one example of a bug the UI could show as "passing" while the API is actually wrong.$L$, $L$Which layer better answers "is the returned data correct?" and why?$L$, $L$Which layer better answers "is the returned data correct?" (Answer: the API layer)$L$, array[$L$I can contrast API and UI testing$L$, $L$I can choose the right layer$L$, $L$I understand how UIs can mask API bugs$L$]
